## Week 12 – Question 10: Product Inventory and Low-Stock Analysis

Problem Statement:

Create a Product Inventory Management System using Java collections. Store each product's ID, name, stock quantity, and price. Update the stock after sales, find products whose stock is below a given threshold, and display products sorted by their remaining stock. Use a collection that supports efficient product lookup by ID.

### Java Program

```

import java.util.*;

class Product {
    int id;
    String name;
    int stock;
    double price;

    Product(int id, String name, int stock, double price) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.stock = stock;
        this.price = price;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();
        HashMap<Integer, Product> map = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int id = sc.nextInt();
            String name = sc.next();
            int stock = sc.nextInt();
            double price = sc.nextDouble();

            map.put(id, new Product(id, name, stock, price));
        }

        int salesId = sc.nextInt();
        int sold = sc.nextInt();

        if (map.containsKey(salesId)) {
            Product p = map.get(salesId);

            if (sold <= p.stock) {
                p.stock -= sold;
            }
        }

        int threshold = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.println("Low Stock Products:");

        for (Product p : map.values()) {
            if (p.stock < threshold) {
                System.out.println(p.id + " " + p.name + " " + p.stock);
            }
        }

        ArrayList<Product> list = new ArrayList<>(map.values());

        list.sort((a, b) -> {
            if (a.stock != b.stock) {
                return a.stock - b.stock;
            }
            return a.id - b.id;
        });

        System.out.println("Products Sorted by Stock:");

        for (Product p : list) {
            System.out.println(p.id + " " + p.name + " " 
                    + p.stock + " " + p.price);
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}

```

### Input

```
3
101 Laptop 10 50000
102 Mouse 5 500
103 Keyboard 8 1500
101 7
5
```

### Output

```
Low Stock Products:
101 Laptop 3
Products Sorted by Stock:
101 Laptop 3 50000.0
102 Mouse 5 500.0
103 Keyboard 8 1500.0
```

### Explanation

1. The program stores product details in a `HashMap` using the product ID as the key.
2. It sells 7 laptops, reducing their stock from 10 to 3.
3. It displays products whose stock is below 5.
4. Finally, it sorts all products by remaining stock in ascending order. If stocks are equal, it sorts by product ID.
